<Layout title="What we model" description="The eight normative ODR-0071 modelling concerns selected for SPDTF, with practical examples, explicit exclusions and a coverage checklist for each context.">
  <h1>What we model and how we check coverage</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    We model meaning, not just the fields in a document. Eight selected concerns from
    source-method ODR-0071 define the required coverage: domain structure, vocabulary,
    classification, validation, mappings, provenance, time and sensitivity. Each context
    must consider them and record its disposition. They are the framework for our work,
    not a suggestion to build eight separate ontologies.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <a href="/semantic-modelling/decision-basis">decision basis</a> records the
    normative source-method subset and the supporting OPDA ADRs. We retain its original
    category numbers so the selection remains traceable. Required coverage does not
    imply implemented or approved candidate meanings.
  </p>

  <h2 id="four-structures">Four structures, four different jobs</h2>
  <table>
    <caption>Do not collapse these structures into one hierarchy</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Structure</th><th scope="col">Count</th><th scope="col">Purpose</th><th scope="col">Output</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Semantic outputs</th><td>6</td><td>The content practitioners review.</td><td>Glossary, dictionary, taxonomies, vocabularies, resources and relationships.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Workshop themes</th><td>11</td><td>Plain-language prompts for finding omissions.</td><td>Questions and evidence under four lenses.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Formal concerns</th><td>8</td><td>A consistent technical completeness checklist.</td><td>One assessment per concern for each context package.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Dispositions</th><td>4</td><td>A recorded ownership decision for each concern.</td><td><code>model here</code>, <code>reuse shared</code>, <code>boundary contribution</code> or <code>not applicable</code>.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Controlled vocabulary and taxonomy are two workshop prompts but one formal concern.
    Governance and compliance is an operating concern, not a seventh semantic output.
    The common boundary is an architectural agreement, not a ninth ontology concern.
  </p>
  <p>
    These structures also differ from <a href="/semantic-modelling/bounded-contexts">semantic
    contexts</a>, which identify where a meaning belongs and who can change it.
    Classification metadata offers independent ways to find and organise that content;
    it does not assign ownership. A conveyancing concept can appear in several search
    categories while keeping one semantic home. None of these structures is a class
    inheritance tree, a folder specification or a requirement for one file per category.
  </p>

  <h2 id="workshop">Four lenses and eleven workshop themes</h2>
  <table>
    <caption>Participant-facing completeness prompts</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Lens</th><th scope="col">Themes</th><th scope="col">Questions to ask</th><th scope="col">Formal relationship</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Meaning</th><td>Domain structure; Controlled vocabulary; Taxonomy; Classification</td><td>What things, words, groupings and classifications matter to your work?</td><td>Maps to the first three formal concerns.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Trust</th><td>Governance and compliance; Provenance and quality; Sensitivity and access</td><td>Who supplied the fact, what can it support, and who may use it for which purpose?</td><td>Governance is operating context; the other two map to formal concerns.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Correctness</th><td>Validation constraints; Time and history</td><td>What must be present for this use, and how do state and recorded time change?</td><td>Maps to validation and temporal concerns.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Exchange</th><td>Cross-domain mappings; Common ontology</td><td>What crosses a boundary, and which small meanings must be shared?</td><td>Mapping is formal; the common boundary is architectural.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="formal">Eight formal ontology concerns</h2>
  <table>
    <caption>Each concern receives one recorded disposition</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Source category and record</th><th scope="col">Concern</th><th scope="col">Business question</th><th scope="col">Example evidence</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">1 · ODR-0071a</th><td>Domain structure</td><td>What things, identities, relationships and rules matter here?</td><td>Scenarios, definitions, examples and counterexamples.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">2 · ODR-0071b</th><td>Vocabulary and taxonomy</td><td>Which terms, values, codes and broader/narrower structures are governed?</td><td>Code lists, glossaries, category trees and stewardship records.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">5 · ODR-0071e</th><td>Classification metadata</td><td>Which subject, lifecycle, regulatory and status facets are needed?</td><td>Search, reporting and operational classification needs.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">7 · ODR-0071g</th><td>Validation and constraints</td><td>Which completeness, consistency and business expectations can be tested?</td><td>Positive and negative examples with agreed rules.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">8 · ODR-0071h</th><td>Cross-domain mappings</td><td>Which meaning-preserving translations are needed at boundaries?</td><td>Paired definitions, examples and qualified comparison evidence.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">9 · ODR-0071i</th><td>Provenance and quality</td><td>What source, attribution, derivation, confidence and quality evidence is required?</td><td>Source registers, lineage, quality measures and authority statements.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">10 · ODR-0071j</th><td>Temporal state and history</td><td>How are valid time, recorded time, states and version chains represented?</td><td>Lifecycle examples and correction histories.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">11 · ODR-0071k</th><td>Access control and data sensitivity</td><td>Which sensitivity, purpose, consent, role and authorisation semantics apply?</td><td>Policy and participant scenarios; never runtime access rules alone.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Cross-domain mappings are Category 8 in the adopted completeness framework: a
    cross-cutting bridge concern, not a ninth content ontology. The
    <a href="/semantic-modelling/evidence-and-mappings#cross-context-mappings">mapping method</a>
    explains the human decision gate, SKOS predicates and the selected SSSOM mapping-record
    discipline. A required mapping method is distinct from populated, reviewed mappings.
  </p>

  <h2 id="domain-structure">1. Domain structure: what exists and how it connects</h2>
  <p>
    Begin with identifiable things and meaningful distinctions. A physical property,
    a legal title, an inspection and the report describing it answer different
    questions. Decide what makes each the same thing over time, which attributes
    belong to it, and which relationships connect it to others. A person acting as
    a buyer illustrates why a temporary role is different from an enduring identity.
    The <a href="/semantic-modelling/identity-roles-and-phases">identity, roles and
    phases guide</a> develops these choices. Source-method ODR-0071a supplies this
    domain-structure concern; workbook nesting does not decide the answer.
  </p>

  <h2 id="vocabulary-taxonomy">2. Vocabulary and taxonomy: govern the choices</h2>
  <p>
    Define the terms people use and the values systems exchange. “No”, “not known”
    and “not applicable” require separate meanings where collapsing them would lose
    information. A governed vocabulary records preferred wording, alternatives,
    definitions, codes and membership; a taxonomy organises concepts using explicit
    broader and narrower relations. We use SKOS for this concern under ODR-0071b.
    A code list is not a reason to create a class for every value, and a list of
    properties being sold is an open population of resources, not a controlled vocabulary.
  </p>

  <h2 id="classification-metadata">5. Classification metadata: organise without redefining</h2>
  <p>
    Readers need to find model content by subject, status and other useful perspectives.
    These classifications describe the content without changing what it is. An
    inspection concept might be relevant to condition, valuation and lending review;
    those labels do not create three inspection classes or transfer the concept's
    semantic ownership. ODR-0071e makes classification an explicit concern rather than
    allowing navigation folders to become an accidental ontology. Record what each
    classification axis means and who maintains it. Keep it distinct from the business
    value sets and taxonomies governed under Category 2.
  </p>

  <h2 id="validation-constraints">7. Validation and constraints: state what a use requires</h2>
  <p>
    A delivery may require an inspection date, a report identifier or an amount with
    its currency. Those expectations belong in explicit, testable rules rather than
    assumptions hidden inside a form. ODR-0071g governs this concern; SHACL supplies
    the constraint language. Record the relevant profile, the business reason and
    examples that should pass or fail. A requirement for one exchange is not
    automatically a universal fact about every resource. The
    <a href="/semantic-modelling/validation">validation guide</a> separates those
    obligations from inference and from a judgement that the source information is true.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cross-domain-mappings">8. Cross-domain mappings: connect the differences</h2>
  <p>
    When a lending context receives survey information, it needs to know which local
    meaning the supplied term represents. Start with the
    <a href="/semantic-modelling/context-maps">context map</a> to identify the
    relationship and responsibilities between communities. Then record any term-level
    correspondence separately, including direction, justification and source versions.
    ODR-0071h governs this cross-cutting concern, supported by the selected mapping
    rules and SSSOM/SEMAPV record discipline. A similar label is a reason to investigate,
    not evidence of equivalence. A justified decision not to map is also useful information.
  </p>

  <h2 id="provenance-quality">9. Provenance and quality: explain what supports a claim</h2>
  <p>
    Separate the thing being described, the statement about it and the source that
    supports that statement. A seller's answer and a surveyor's observation may have
    different scope, dates and evidential value. ODR-0071i requires those distinctions
    to remain inspectable, including attribution and derivation. Quality measures
    should say what was assessed and against which expectation: completeness is not
    accuracy, and confidence is not approval. PROV-O and DQV provide reusable language
    for relevant provenance and quality constructs; precise term use follows the
    <a href="/semantic-modelling/linked-data-languages">linked-data language profile</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="temporal-history">10. Temporal state and history: distinguish the clocks</h2>
  <p>
    A report issued on Thursday can describe an inspection performed on Monday. A
    correction entered next month may apply to an earlier period. ODR-0071j requires
    the model to distinguish when something happened or applied from when it was
    recorded, where the use case needs both. Preserve relevant versions and changes
    rather than overwriting the past with the latest value. A lifecycle phase belongs
    to the thing passing through that phase; a programme review status describes the
    model's standing. Neither should silently stand in for the other.
  </p>

  <h2 id="access-sensitivity">11. Access and sensitivity: describe permitted use precisely</h2>
  <p>
    Model the meanings needed to explain sensitive information, purpose, consent and
    authorisation. For example, permission to use a document for one stated review
    should not be interpreted as permission to publish it or reuse it for an unrelated
    purpose. ODR-0071k makes these distinctions part of the semantic account, using
    the relevant DPV and policy vocabulary profile. The ontology describes the
    agreement; deployed access controls enforce it. A sensitivity label alone neither
    grants access nor proves that an implementation protects the data correctly.
  </p>

  <h2 id="excluded">What this ontology scope excludes</h2>
  <p>
    We exclude source Categories 3 (process modelling), 4 (service architecture),
    6 (governance and compliance as an ontology), 12 (capability and intent),
    13 (source mapping) and 14 (data product). Programme governance still operates,
    applications still need architecture and historical schema mappings remain evidence.
    Those needs do not expand this domain-model mandate. In particular, a coverage
    link from a Property Pack requirement to a model construct records traceability;
    it does not turn executable source-to-target transformation into a retained category.
  </p>

  <h2 id="dispositions">Four allowed dispositions</h2>
  <table>
    <caption>Disposition guide for each formal concern</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Disposition</th><th scope="col">Use when</th><th scope="col">Required record</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row"><code>model here</code></th><td>The context owns substantive domain meaning.</td><td>Owner, evidence, candidate constructs and review questions.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row"><code>reuse shared</code></th><td>A common or externally governed profile already meets the need.</td><td>Exact version, terms, mechanism and conformance boundary.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row"><code>boundary contribution</code></th><td>The context supplies requirements or mappings to Interoperability.</td><td>Consumer need, affected contexts and proposed agreement.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row"><code>not applicable</code></th><td>The concern genuinely does not apply to this scope.</td><td>A reviewable rationale and re-open trigger.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="apply">How to apply the checklist</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Run the eleven themes as a facilitated conversation; capture unanswered questions.</li>
    <li>Assess each of the eight concerns against attributed evidence and competency questions.</li>
    <li>Choose exactly one disposition per concern and name the accountable owner.</li>
    <li>Link any modelled concern to the relevant semantic outputs and technical artefacts.</li>
    <li>Record gaps, dissent, dependencies and re-open triggers instead of filling them with assumptions.</li>
    <li>Repeat the assessment when evidence, scope, standards profile or candidate version changes.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Completeness does not mean “model everything.” A well-justified reuse or
    not-applicable decision is better than duplicating shared terms or inventing content
    for a concern that has no evidence.
  </p>
